.TH SUDO 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
sudo \- run one command as root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Verify the caller's own password, confirm they're listed in
.BR /etc/mitos\-sudoers " (one username per line, " # " comments), then run "
.I command
as root with a small, safe environment (HOME/TERM kept, PATH reset,
everything else cleared). Already being root skips both checks.
.SH DELIBERATELY SMALLER THAN REAL SUDO
No per\-command rules, no
.BR NOPASSWD ,
no timestamp\-based "don't ask again" caching, no
.BR \-u " (always targets root). Every one of those is also a place real "
sudoers\-parsing/timestamp\-cache implementations have had genuine CVEs
-- smaller here is deliberate.
.SH SECURITY
.B This has not been independently security-reviewed.
It only functions at all when installed setuid\-root
(owned by root,
.BR "chmod u+s" );
until then it cannot read
.B /etc/shadow
and every attempt fails, which is the safe default.
.B /etc/mitos\-sudoers
itself must be owned by root and writable only by root, or every request
is refused outright -- read
.B common::auth
in the source before installing this setuid anywhere that matters.
Get it reviewed and test it exhaustively in a disposable VM first.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR, and \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. Non-zero on failed authentication, an unauthorized user,
or a usage error -- `sudo` otherwise never returns at all, since a
successful run execs the target command in place of this process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR su (1),
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7)
